00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.800
A resource hierarchy
helps us understand

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.500
how your resources are
going to be organized, which

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.410
is extremely crucial when your
organization is scaling out,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.290
and is a great foundation
to understanding inheritance

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.080
of access control and
organization policies

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.550
for your resources.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.200
To the bottom most
of the hierarchy,

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.960
we have all our resources,
which are essentially

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.630
all the products and services,
like your Compute Engine

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.840
instances, machine
learning APIs, databases,

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.560
and everything else.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.670
This is where your
application really stands.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.700
Resources can only be
parented by projects.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.579
And this becomes the first
coping mechanism in your Google

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:21.500
Cloud resource hierarchy.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.120
You need to have at
least one project

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.790
to get started with
building your resources.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.730
It is a best practice that you
segregate your projects based

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.410
on your environment, such
as development, test,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.510
and production.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.600
This way, the flow is kept
clean, easy to manage,

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.400
debugged, and scaled.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.010
Right above projects
we have folders.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.730
These folders can contain other
projects, as well as folders.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.130
This provides an additional
grouping mechanism

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.790
to help keep your resources
and accesses restricted

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:53.240
maybe department-wise,
team-wise, and so on.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.670
Maybe you wouldn't want to
research team's resources

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.900
to be accessed by the
other departments.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:01.640
So you can keep them
isolated, yet under one roof.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.490
And that one roof
is an organization.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.030
This is the umbrella
for your entire company

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.860
and acts as the root node.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.070
It is a prerequisite for you to
have an organization in order

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.050
to use folders.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:15.940
Now that we have a
broad understanding

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.840
of the entire
resource hierarchy,

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.810
let's try and understand
an organization

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:23.290
in a little more detail
and why it's so important.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.560
Organizations provide central
visibility and control

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.190
over every resource that
belongs to that organization.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:34.390
The IAM access control policies
applied to an organization

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.300
apply throughout the hierarchy
to all the resources.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.440
It is not required or mandatory
to have an organization,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.570
but some features
of resource manager

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:46.180
will not be usable without one.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.970
If you are a Google
Workspace or Cloud Identity

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.640
user, when you create
a Google Cloud project,

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.770
an organization is
automatically provisioned.

